---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Linking Your Tools - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="requirements-gathering" subChapterId="linking-your-tools">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Before you start gathering requirements with AI, connect it to the tools you already use. AI assistants are dramatically more useful when they can see your actual documentation, tickets, and project context instead of you copy-pasting everything by hand.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Why?
			</h3>

			<p>
				Without tool integration, every AI conversation starts from zero. You describe your project, paste in requirements, explain your ticketing structure, and then do it all again next conversation. Linking your tools lets the AI pull that context directly, which means less repetition and better results.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Point It at Your Files
			</h3>

			<p>
				The simplest and most powerful starting point: give your AI tool access to a directory that contains your templates, rules, and documentation. If your team keeps requirement templates, definition-of-done checklists, or architecture decision records in a shared folder or repo, point your AI assistant directly at that directory. If they don't, now is the time to start!
			</p>

			<p>
				In Claude Code, this happens automatically when you work within a project directory. Cursor and similar editors also pick up files in your workspace. The key insight is that the AI can read and follow your existing standards if it can see them. Instead of explaining "we always include acceptance criteria in this format," just let it read the template file.
			</p>

			<p>
				Claude Cowork works a little differently. Rather than opening a local project directory, it runs in the cloud (across web, desktop, and mobile) and reaches your context through folders you grant it plus connectors and plugins to the tools you already use. You choose exactly which folders and tools it can touch, and it can carry out longer or scheduled tasks without you sitting there driving it. The same principle still applies: point it at the documentation and templates that define how your team works.
			</p>

			<p>Your project can also contain skills that help the AI better interact the way you want it to. This is a powerful way to get the AI to follow your team's conventions and processes.</p>

			<p>
				Getting this context in place is also the groundwork for two approaches we'll cover in a later section: requirements-first and documentation-first development, where the specs and docs you write up front become the primary input the AI builds from.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Create a dedicated folder (like <code>docs/</code>) with your team's templates, style guides, and process documents. When you start an AI session, reference these files explicitly: "Follow the template in docs/requirement-template.md." Over time, this becomes your team's shared AI playbook.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				Online Tool Integrations
			</h3>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Confluence</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Your project documentation, architecture decisions, and team wikis. Claude (via the official Atlassian connector) and Cursor can both connect to Confluence, with MCP servers as a fallback for other tools. Once connected, you can reference pages directly in your prompts instead of copy-pasting content.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Jira / Linear</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Your ticketing system holds a wealth of context: acceptance criteria, linked issues, past discussions. Jira connects through the same official Atlassian connector; Linear and others connect via their own connectors or MCP servers. Once linked, you can say "look at PROJ-1234" instead of manually copying ticket details into every prompt.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Google Drive + Gemini</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						If your team works in Google Docs or Sheets, Gemini has native access to your Drive files. You can ask Gemini to pull context from specific documents, summarize meeting notes, or cross-reference requirements across multiple files in your workspace.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<Callout variant="info" title="Start small">
				<p>
					You don't need every integration on day one. Start with wherever your requirements actually live, usually your documentation platform and ticket tracker. Add more as you find yourself repeatedly copy-pasting from the same sources.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Don't connect every tool at once. Each integration adds context that the AI has to process, and too many can fill up the context window, leaving less room for your actual conversation. Link the tools you'll actively use for the task at hand and add more as needed.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				A Note on Security
			</h3>

			<p>
				When connecting tools, be thoughtful about what you're giving access to.
			</p>

